#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060575} :activity/id #uuid "1f56b8ef-868f-57e2-ae40-b8e705105170" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050366 :topic/id #uuid "4264930a-5f20-54f0-ad2a-c3ad45c03658" :topic/math-academy-id 250} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060989 :tutorial/content "A **conditional statement** (or **implication**) is a mathematical statement that takes the form\n\n$\\qquad$ *$P$ implies $Q,$*\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$\\qquad$ *if $P,$ then $Q.$*\n\nSymbolically, it can be written as $P \\Rightarrow Q,$ where $P$ is called the **antecedent** (or **hypothesis**) and $Q$ is called the **consequent** (or **conclusion**).\n\nThe truth table for $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is shown below.\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\Rightarrow Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n\nIntuitively, an implication can be viewed as a \"promise\" that whenever $P$ is true, $Q$ is also true. The implication is true unless the promise is broken, i.e., $P$ is true, and $Q$ is false.\n\nWe'll discuss the intuition behind implications in more detail later in the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "a6598728-1780-50b5-af45-ace426318bc6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7977 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62e05d8d-2ee3-5cd5-8592-01e6eef66d71" :step/math-academy-id 154035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059705 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059414 :question/id #uuid "8831a019-f252-5678-9855-6c0f1da89f77" :question/math-academy-id "e-12706"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df9791a3-465d-57e4-8f2e-40fb9fb18d70" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376308}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Truth Values of Implications"} :step/id #uuid "89220a35-670a-5c3b-9161-aa844a949880" :step/math-academy-id 154036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059706 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059413 :question/id #uuid "0b97d670-694b-52e5-aa55-1224985f83a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-12705"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e1c01fd-dea0-5761-9499-d622a34fea3e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050783}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390119}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Truth Table With Compound Statements"} :step/id #uuid "cb82b9e6-f25a-5610-8374-2261c53cde3b" :step/math-academy-id 154039 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061000 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the truth table for $P\\Rightarrow Q.$\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, P \\Rightarrow Q \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n\nWe consider a concrete example to build further intuition behind the truth values in this table.\n\nSuppose your teacher promises that *if* you score at least $90$ on the exam, *then* you will receive an A. In other words, the teacher has promised that a score of at least $90$ on the exam implies that you will receive an A: $$\\textrm{Score at least 90} \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\textrm{Receive an A}$$\n\nNow, the teacher may or may not be lying. The implication is true if the teacher tells the truth and false if the teacher lies.\n\nThe following four situations are possible:\n\n1. You score $90$ and receive an A. In this case, the teacher has told the truth. They have kept the promise. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n2. You score $90$ and do *not* receive an A. In this case, the teacher has lied. They have broken the promise. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n3. You do *not* score $90,$ yet you receive an A. In this case, the teacher has told the truth! The promise does not apply because you did not score at least $90.$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n4. You do *not* score $90$ and do *not* receive an A. Again, the teacher has told the truth. The promise does not apply because you did not score at least $90.$ $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nEach of these situations corresponds to a row in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\textrm{At least 90}$ | $\\textrm{Receive an A}$ | $\\textrm{Score at least 90} \\Rightarrow \\textrm{Receive an A}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{black} \\textrm T$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "6943ab9c-bf27-5dbb-9871-a5647b7d3b74" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8292 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Implication Truth Table"} :step/id #uuid "4b3b0e3b-bca5-585d-999e-a2ea88c36b2b" :step/math-academy-id 154043 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059707 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059412 :question/id #uuid "13e5834d-4d70-5893-a4bf-9927000d522d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12704"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64a3922f-7e74-562f-9bb9-c4a41e912ce7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377885}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Conditional Statements"} :step/id #uuid "ea087540-046f-51b4-a818-16971088b6d6" :step/math-academy-id 154044}] :activity/title "Conditional Statements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059934}]}
